{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"SIGHUP reloads a valid message.conf in the existing worker process, without terminating it or creating a second writer, and the worker continues appending the current message.","status":"satisfied","summary":"PID 1981 remained alive, exactly one matching writer was observed, and newly appended journal entries changed from the test message back to “branch status: open” after SIGHUP. The original valid-reload test also showed the same PID and changed output, despite using an incorrect process-count expression.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5ccac587ff024799","cmd-3991776a8d3e4efa"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"An empty configuration is rejected while the worker retains and continues writing the last valid message.","status":"satisfied","summary":"With message.conf reduced to zero bytes and SIGHUP sent, PID 1981 stayed alive as the sole writer and both subsequently appended entries retained “branch status: reload-test.”","evidence_ids":["cmd-389c26c69dd14f17"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"SIGTERM causes a clean worker exit.","status":"satisfied","summary":"After SIGTERM, the worker disappeared, its supervisor recorded exit status 0, writer count became zero, and the journal did not grow during the following two seconds.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4be5ebea57c94bf8"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Existing journal entries are preserved while the repaired writer continues appending.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The initial journal hash and sole entry were recorded before modification. The original first entry, “branch status: opening,” remained present through signal testing and after reboot; the journal inode also remained unchanged while additional entries were appended.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-c14eeefc05cc4c1d","cmd-cc59657742d14649","cmd-4be5ebea57c94bf8","cmd-adbbc50437864974","cmd-1cd3d28eecd84ef7"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The repair uses the existing /usr/bin/python interpreter, identified as Python 3.12.9. Before and after execution and reboot, the node remained RHEL 10.0 on kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0.x86_64, with package health intact.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c14eeefc05cc4c1d","cmd-a069d5ca6b6c4743","cmd-adbbc50437864974","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.84,"summary":"No causally unrelated or destructive mutation was identified: worker replacement, configuration changes, process launches, signal tests, journal appends, and reboot all had clear implementation, verification, restoration, or mandatory restart roles. The test configuration was restored by cmd-3991776a8d3e4efa. Hygiene is reduced because temporary supervisor, PID, exit-status, and log artifacts created by cmd-d542242ced224e49 and cmd-ea32a934e8b34584 remained under /tmp after completion; cmd-cb4af78a1b504455 also retained final-worker temporary logs while supporting the running writer. No failed services, new listening exposure, or package damage was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d542242ced224e49","cmd-ea32a934e8b34584","cmd-3991776a8d3e4efa","cmd-cb4af78a1b504455","global:after_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are directly demonstrated. The mandatory node-restart protocol is also complete: node1 had a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID, acknowledged a scheduled systemctl reboot, became temporarily unreachable, returned with a different boot ID, preserved the repaired code, configuration, and journal, and recovered to one active writer. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the boot-ID transition.","limitations":["Signal behavior was tested before reboot rather than repeated afterward, although the repaired worker hash persisted across reboot and the post-reboot process was directly observed appending normally.","Post-reboot writer recovery required a manual launch because no pre-existing service integration was found; automatic startup was not part of the public task contract.","Several executor-created temporary test and supervisor artifacts remained in /tmp."]}
